### Director's diagnosis (Director's correct observation)

Empirical confirmation via `webapp/scripts/check-img-metadata.ts` + `read-plan-content.ts`:
- 13 APPROVED Plans for SS-S15-E01 ALL have diverse `camera_intent.angle` (WIDE/MEDIUM/CLOSE/MEDIUM_WIDE/CLOSE_UP)
- Each has explicit `sub_area_variation` motivation «distinct from sibling shots SHxx to avoid flat-plate repetition»
- Each prompt body starts with concrete framing language (SH02: «Tight close-up on the warm tan wooden foot of the mirror_vanity»)
- BUT 20 APPROVED IMG-episode_ref outputs are visually identical layout (bed-left / vanity-center / bookcase-right), except SH20+ (wall-hole shot, Director-driven exception)
- IMG metadata.shot_reference has NO `camera_angle`/`sub_area` field — Designer decisions don't survive into the rendered asset's metadata.

**Root cause confirmed** in [openai-edits-multi.ts:13-17](webapp/lib/agents/providers/openai-edits-multi.ts:13):
> «No `strength` parameter is exposed by the API → identity is locked hard, emotion/action prompt has weaker influence than Flux Pro Ultra. Per-reference weight not supported → all refs contribute equally.»

The single canonical Location Bible reference image is a fixed-viewpoint render of the bedroom. It's passed as one of N equal-weighted image references in every multi-edit call. Text prompt «CLOSE-UP» can't override the visual layout the location reference carries.

